  Line 13:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Transcription and RNA',text:'RNA polymerases make RNA using DNA as a template. RNA uses ribose and usually uracil (U) in place of thymine. Many RNAs are translated into proteins, while others perform structural, catalytic, regulatory, or guide roles. In eukaryotes, many messenger RNAs are processed (including splicing, capping, and polyadenylation); bacteria commonly couple transcription and translation. Not every gene encodes a protein.'},

