  Line 16:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Mutation, recombination, and repair',text:'A mutation is a heritable change in genetic material. Variants can arise during DNA copying or after damage from internal processes or environmental agents. Outcomes range from neutral to harmful or beneficial depending on context; many effects are uncertain. Cells have repair pathways, including mismatch repair and pathways for base damage and DNA breaks. Recombination exchanges DNA between molecules and is important in meiosis, repair, and microbial gene exchange.'},

  Line 17:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Epigenetics and chromatin',text:'In cells with chromatin, DNA is packaged with proteins such as histones. Chemical modifications to DNA or histones, nucleosome positioning, and regulatory RNAs can influence gene activity without changing the underlying base sequence. Some such states persist through cell division; others are transient or reset. “Epigenetic” mechanisms vary widely across life and should not be treated as a single switch or as automatically inherited across generations.'},

  Line 18:  {category:'Genomes across life',title:'Genome diversity across organisms',text:'DNA genomes occur in cellular life, but genome organization varies enormously: genome size is not a direct measure of organismal complexity. Bacteria and archaea often carry compact genomes and may exchange genes; eukaryotes often have introns and extensive regulatory and repetitive DNA. Some viruses use DNA genomes (others use RNA), and viruses depend on host cells to reproduce. Mitochondria and chloroplasts retain small genomes descended from ancient bacterial ancestors.'},

