  Line 12:  {category:'Molecular foundations',title:'Genome, genes, and chromosomes',text:'A genome is an organism’s complete genetic material, including protein-coding genes and many kinds of noncoding sequence. A gene is a DNA region whose expression produces a functional RNA or, through RNA, a protein; gene boundaries and meanings vary by context. Chromosomes package long DNA molecules with proteins and other molecules. Most bacteria have one main circular chromosome, while eukaryotes typically have multiple linear nuclear chromosomes plus DNA in mitochondria and, in plants and algae, chloroplasts. These are broad patterns, not universal rules.'},

  Line 13:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Replication and inheritance',text:'Before many cells divide, DNA polymerases copy DNA using each parental strand as a template. Replication is generally semiconservative: each daughter molecule contains one old strand and one newly synthesized strand. Origins, enzymes, proofreading, and chromosome-end maintenance differ among organisms. DNA is transmitted through cell division and reproduction, but inheritance is shaped by recombination, chromosome segregation, developmental biology, and environment—not by sequence alone.'},

  Line 14:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Transcription and RNA',text:'RNA polymerases make RNA using DNA as a template. RNA uses ribose and usually uracil (U) in place of thymine. Many RNAs are translated into proteins, while others perform structural, catalytic, regulatory, or guide roles. In eukaryotes, many messenger RNAs are processed (including splicing, capping, and polyadenylation); bacteria commonly couple transcription and translation. Not every gene encodes a protein.'},

  Line 15:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Translation and the genetic code',text:'Ribosomes read messenger RNA in three-base codons and, with transfer RNAs, assemble amino acids into a polypeptide. The standard genetic code maps most codons to amino acids and includes start and stop signals. It is nearly universal, but not absolutely: some organisms and organelles use variant codes. Proteins then fold, may be modified, and interact with other molecules; the DNA sequence alone does not specify every detail of a cell’s final state.'},

  Line 16:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Noncoding DNA and gene regulation',text:'Noncoding DNA includes regulatory sequences, introns, structural regions, repeated elements, and sequences with unknown or context-dependent functions. Promoters and enhancers can influence when, where, and how strongly genes are transcribed, often through transcription factors and chromatin state. “Noncoding” does not mean “nonfunctional,” and sequence conservation or biochemical activity alone does not prove a specific biological function.'},

  Line 17:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Mutation, recombination, and repair',text:'A mutation is a heritable change in genetic material. Variants can arise during DNA copying or after damage from internal processes or environmental agents. Outcomes range from neutral to harmful or beneficial depending on context; many effects are uncertain. Cells have repair pathways, including mismatch repair and pathways for base damage and DNA breaks. Recombination exchanges DNA between molecules and is important in meiosis, repair, and microbial gene exchange.'},

  Line 18:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Epigenetics and chromatin',text:'In cells with chromatin, DNA is packaged with proteins such as histones. Chemical modifications to DNA or histones, nucleosome positioning, and regulatory RNAs can influence gene activity without changing the underlying base sequence. Some such states persist through cell division; others are transient or reset. “Epigenetic” mechanisms vary widely across life and should not be treated as a single switch or as automatically inherited across generations.'},

  Line 19:  {category:'Genomes across life',title:'Genome diversity across organisms',text:'DNA genomes occur in cellular life, but genome organization varies enormously: genome size is not a direct measure of organismal complexity. Bacteria and archaea often carry compact genomes and may exchange genes; eukaryotes often have introns and extensive regulatory and repetitive DNA. Some viruses use DNA genomes (others use RNA), and viruses depend on host cells to reproduce. Mitochondria and chloroplasts retain small genomes descended from ancient bacterial ancestors.'},

  Line 21:  {category:'Studying DNA',title:'Genome editing and biotechnology',text:'CRISPR-associated systems use guide molecules and proteins to target nucleic-acid sequences; related tools can cut, regulate, or otherwise modify genetic material. Outcomes depend on the organism, delivery, target, repair processes, and verification. Genome editing is distinct from simply “rewriting a gene,” and off-target effects, mosaicism, ecological consequences, and ethical considerations require careful assessment. This Codex is informational, not an experimental protocol.'},

  Line 22:  {category:'Human genetics and society',title:'Human genetic variation',text:'Two human genomes are overwhelmingly similar, yet millions of common and rare variants exist across populations. Most individual traits reflect many genetic variants and environmental influences, and categories such as ancestry or population are complex and do not map neatly onto social race. Genetic association is not proof of causation. Responsible interpretation considers consent, privacy, uncertainty, representation, and potential harms.'},

  Line 23:  {category:'Human genetics and society',title:'Ancient and environmental DNA',text:'DNA can be recovered from historical remains, sediments, water, and other environmental samples, but it is often fragmented and chemically damaged. Contamination controls and careful sampling are essential. Environmental DNA can help detect organisms without observing them directly, while ancient DNA can illuminate past populations and ecosystems. Neither type of evidence is automatically complete or free of interpretation limits.'}

